#          and vespene >= 100, even before the hallucination returned scouting data.
#          This left the bot with gas banked but 0 gas income, and the zealot/charge
#          production couldn't use the gas fast enough, while stalkers couldn't be
#          produced because SEQUENCE 4 was deadlocked.
#          FIX: Don't pull gas workers until hallucination scouting confirms the
#          enemy tech path (no roach warren seen and halluc results are back, or
#          spire is seen).
#      (d) Only ONE hallucination was ever sent in PvZ. `scout_attempted=True`
#          permanently blocked re-casting. No `_pvz_spire_seen` flag existed.
#          FIX: Added `_pvz_spire_seen` flag, `_check_spire()` detection, and
#          continuous hallucination re-casting in PvZ until spire is scouted.
#          After hallucination expires, `scout_attempted` is reset for re-cast.
# =========================================================================
#  59. PVZ EMERGENCY BATTERY PYLON BUG + DIRECTION BUG (RECURRING):
#      (a) _pvz_calculate_emergency_battery_positions used to insert PYLON
#          positions before battery positions in the build queue. These pylon
#          positions were calculated 2-3 units from the wallpylon (towards
#          gw1/cyber/gw2), placing them LITERALLY ADJACENT to the wallpylon.
#          The wallpylon has a power radius of 6.5, so all battery positions
#          (3-6 units from wallpylon) are already powered. The emergency pylons
#          were completely redundant -- they wasted 100 minerals each, pulled a
#          probe off the mineral line, and placed a useless pylon right next to
#          the wallpylon at the natural instead of around the main base where
#          flower production builds pylons.
#          FIX: Removed the entire pylon position calculation. Only battery
#          positions are calculated now. DO NOT re-add emergency pylons here.
#      (b) The battery direction vector was (wall_mid - ref_pos), which goes
#          FROM the building TOWARDS the wall midpoint (OUTSIDE the wall,
#          enemy-facing side). This placed shield batteries on the OUTSIDE of
#          the wall where enemies can attack them. The intent was "-3.5" units
#          -- go AWAY from wall midpoint by 3.5 (INTERIOR side, safe side).
#          FIX: Negated the direction to (ref_pos - wall_mid), which goes AWAY
#          from the wall midpoint, placing batteries behind the wall buildings
#          on the safe/interior side.
# =========================================================================
#  44. ALREADY_PENDING IS UNRELIABLE FOR BUILD TRACKING: already_pending()
#      returns True on the SAME FRAME that builder.build() is called, even
#      before the building physically appears. If the probe gets interrupted
#      (attacked, mining system reassigns it, move command from another system),
#      the build order is cancelled but already_pending() already returned True
#      on that frame. The state machine sees already_pending=True and advances
#      to the next state, releasing the probe back to mining. The building
#      never gets built.
#      CORRECT APPROACH: Wait for self.structures(TYPE) to return a result
#      (the building has physically appeared in game state) before advancing
#      state or releasing the builder probe. already_pending() should ONLY be
#      used as a guard to prevent double-ordering (e.g., "don't order a 2nd
#      forge while one is pending"), NEVER as confirmation that a building
#      is under construction.
#      Also: When a build command is issued, keep builder_current_task and
#      builder_task_target set until the structure physically appears. This
#      lets is_builder_busy() correctly track the builder state and prevents
#      manage_flower_production from stealing the probe or re-ordering.
#      Example bug: State 70.6 issues builder.build(FORGE), then immediately
#      sets builder_current_task=None. If the probe gets interrupted, the
#      forge is never built but the state machine already moved on.
#
#  45. BUILDER IDLE + FAR FROM PYLON TARGET = SUPPLY BLOCK: In
#      is_builder_busy(), the pylon section used to return True (busy) when
#      builder.distance_to(target) > 3, assuming the builder was "en route".
#      But if the builder is IDLE (not moving) and far from the target, the
#      build order was lost/cancelled/interrupted -- another system gave the
#      probe a move order that completed, or the build command failed and the
#      probe became idle at a different position. Without this check,
#      is_builder_busy() returns True forever, blocking ALL flower production,
#      which stops pylon building entirely. The supply cap stays frozen while
#      the army keeps producing units, eventually hitting a permanent supply
#      block. In the match log, the builder was idle at (149.55, 137.75)
#      while the pylon target was (139, 134) -- 11.2 distance away -- for
#      100+ seconds, causing supply to stay at 38/39 the entire time.
#      FIX: When the builder is idle AND far from the pylon target (>3 dist),
#      clear the builder task so manage_flower_production can reassign it.
#      Also check for hijacked orders (HARVEST_GATHER/RETURN/ATTACK while
#      far from target) -- same as the shield_battery section already does.
#
#  46. PVT RAMP DEPOT VISION GATE: When stalkers focus-fire a supply depot
#      at the enemy main ramp, attack(depot_target) fails silently if the
#      stalker is at the bottom of the ramp without high-ground vision. The
#      stalker stands at max attack range (6) unable to fire because it can't
#      see the depot on high ground. The attack order is issued every frame
#      but the weapon never fires. This wastes the entire push -- stalkers
#      stand below the ramp indefinitely while the depot blocks the path.
#      FIX: Before issuing attack(depot_target), check if the stalker is
#      far from the depot (distance > 6, likely at the bottom of the ramp).
#      If so, use move(depot_target) instead of attack(depot_target). The
#      move command doesn't require vision -- it physically pushes the
#      stalker up the ramp toward the depot position. Once the stalker is
#      within 6 distance (on the ramp / high ground with vision), switch to
#      attack(). This applies to ALL depot focus-fire sections: proxy_rax
#      Priority -1 and Priority 3, proxy_hidden_tech Priority 0, and the
#      reaper_response ramp depot section.
#
#  49. PVT SHIELD BATTERY ENERGY RECHARGE + STALKER PRODUCTION BEFORE CHARGE:
#      TWO ISSUES during PvT defense:
#      (a) When the enemy rushes our base and we defend with stalkers kiting
#          back to the shield battery, the battery can run out of energy (< 10).
#          Without energy, the battery can't recharge stalker shields, and our
#          stalkers die instead of healing. The Nexus has an Energy Recharge
#          ability (ENERGYRECHARGE_ENERGYRECHARGE) that can refill the battery.
#          When nexus energy >= 50 and any shield battery has energy < 10, we
#          should use Energy Recharge on it. This was only implemented for the
#          proxy marauder path, but it's needed for ALL PvT paths (reaper
#          response, gateway first, etc.) because shield battery defense is
#          universal in PvT.
#      (b) The general production path (SEQUENCE 4, after air threat and
#          emergency 4-gate blocks) produced ONLY zealots with no PvT check.
#          In PvT, zealots without charge are useless -- they're too slow to
#          catch kiting Terran bio and get picked apart. Stalkers should be
#          produced instead until charge starts researching. After charge
#          starts, chargelots are effective and can be produced normally.
#          FIX: Added a universal PvT energy recharge on shield battery at
#          the top of the vs_terran chrono section (before proxy marauder).
#          Also added a check in the general production path: in PvT, produce
#          stalkers when charge hasn't started, zealots only after charge starts.
#
#  50. PVP VOIDRAY BUILD: STATE ADVANCES BEFORE BUILDING PHYSICALLY APPEARS:
#      The PvP voidray state machine advanced to the next state immediately
#      after calling probe.build(), without waiting for the structure to
#      physically appear in self.structures(). This is the same class of bug
#      as note #44 (already_pending is unreliable). If the probe gets
#      interrupted (e.g., retaliating against an enemy worker scout, or
#      another system issuing a move command), the build order is lost but
#      the state already advanced past it. The building is never built and
#      the entire build order is permanently broken.
#      In the match log: The probe was fighting an enemy worker at the natural.
#      probe.build(NEXUS) was issued at t=54.8s, but the probe's ATTACK order
#      overrode the build. The state immediately advanced to 2 (GW1), which
#      advanced to 4 (cyber), which got stuck forever because there was no
#      nexus for economy. The bot sat on 1 nexus and 1 gateway for 6+ minutes
#      accumulating 5000+ minerals with no production.
#      FIX: Added half-states (1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5) that wait for the
#      structure to physically appear in self.structures() before advancing.
#      If the build order is lost (structure not pending AND not placed),
#      the half-state falls back to retry the build. States 5, 6, 7 already
#      had the correct pattern (they stay in the same state until the
#      structure exists, then advance on the "already exists" check).
#
#  51. PVZ HATCH-FIRST SHIELD BATTERY BUILDER BOUNCE / STUCK:
#      TWO ISSUES causing shield battery builders to bounce between
#      mining and building instead of actually building:
#      (a) 3rd base shield battery (state 67.6): When the pylon builder
#          was released after building the 3rd pylon, hatch_pylon_builder_tag
#          was set to None. The battery builder fallback path (get_free_worker)
#          set builder_probe_tag but NOT hatch_pylon_builder_tag. Every frame,
#          the code checked hatch_pylon_builder_tag first (None → "probe died"),
#          grabbed a new free worker (because the previous one was now excluded
#          by builder_probe_tag), and set builder_probe_tag to the new worker.
#          The old worker went back to mining. Two probes bounced endlessly:
#          one moving to the battery position, one mining, swapping every frame.
#          The "3rd battery builder probe died" message spammed every frame.
#          FIX: Also set hatch_pylon_builder_tag when the fallback worker is
#          assigned, so the mining system excludes the worker via away_tags.
#          Also added a check for builder_probe_tag when hatch_pylon_builder_tag
#          is None (to find the previously-assigned worker instead of grabbing
#          a new one). And added worker.stop() before worker.move() to cancel
#          any active mining orders that the mining system may have just issued.
#      (b) Nat shield battery (state 67.65): When reclaiming the nexus_first
#          probe or using get_free_worker, the probe may still have active
#          HARVEST_GATHER or HARVEST_RETURN orders. The builder.move() command
#          is issued but the mining system overrides it on the next frame because
#          the probe's current order is still a mining command. The probe bounces
#          between moving to the battery position and returning to mine.
#          FIX: Added worker.stop() before builder.move() to cancel any active
#          mining/gather orders, ensuring the move command takes effect.
# =========================================================================
#  83. PVZ ROBO IDLE: RESOURCE STARVATION FROM WARPGATE PRODUCTION:
#      When a roach warren was spotted in PvZ, the robotics facility would
#      sit idle for minutes while warpgates produced zealots/stalkers. The
#      timeline showed: robo built at 280s, observer didn't start until 334s
#      (54s delay), immortal #1 at 352s, immortal #2 at 473s (121s gap!),
#      and then ZERO more immortals despite 3 idle robos. Root causes:
#      (a) EXECUTION ORDER: manage_army_production() ran BEFORE
#          manage_pvz_roach_response(). Warpgates drained all resources
#          (especially minerals for zealots at 100/0 each) before the robo
#          could claim resources for observer (25/75) or immortal (275/100).
#          With 9 warpgates cycling zealots, minerals were always at 0 when
#          the robo production code ran. FIX: Moved manage_pvz_roach_response
#          to mid_game_phase() BEFORE manage_army_production() so robo gets
#          first dibs on resources every frame.
#      (b) NO RESOURCE RESERVATION: Even with execution order fixed, if the
#          robo couldn't produce on frame N (not enough resources), warpgates
#          on frame N+1 would spend the newly-accumulated resources before the
#          robo could claim them. FIX: Added robo resource reservation in
#          SEQUENCE 4: when idle robos need observer, reserve 25/75; when
#          they need immortal, reserve 275/100 per idle robo. Warpgates skip
#          production if it would dip below the reserve threshold.
#      (c) GAS RESERVATION FOR STALKERS: Before charge starts, SEQUENCE 4
#          produces stalkers (50 gas each) which directly competes with
#          observer (75 gas) and immortal (100 gas) for the limited gas.
#          FIX: Added gas reservation check in both the roach defense
#          section (stalker production) and SEQUENCE 4 (_pvz_roach_mode
#          stalkers) — skip stalker production if it would dip below the
#          gas needed for the next robo unit.
#      (d) INSUFFICIENT GAS GEYSERS: With 3 nexuses and only 2 geysers,
#          gas income was ~200/min, barely enough for 1 immortal per 30s.
#          Against roach timing attacks, we need faster immortal production.
#          FIX: Added gas geyser construction in manage_pvz_roach_response —
#          target 2 geysers per nexus (up to 6 geysers for 3 bases = ~600
#          gas/min). Gas transfer (mineral dump) is already blocked when
#          roach warren is spotted, so these geysers will be fully utilized.
#
#  84. PVZ ROACH-BANE BUILD ORDER: GAS AFTER GW2 (NOT BEFORE):
#      The roach-bane build order was Nexus > GW1 > Gas1+2(parallel) > GW2.
#      Gas started at ~75s while GW2 didn't start until ~93s. This meant gas
#      was being built before the second gateway, delaying gateway production
#      (150 minerals spent on each assimilator instead of on GW2). Against a
#      roach rush timing, having 2 gateways up ASAP is critical for stalker
#      production. FIX: Changed build order to Nexus > GW1 > GW2 > Gas1 > Gas2.
#      Gas is now triggered AFTER GW2 is verified (state 81.5 sets
#      _rb_gas_triggered). Gas1 must complete before Gas2 starts (sequential,
#      not parallel). The builder probe handles GW2 > Cyber in sequence while
#      mining workers handle Gas1 > Gas2 after GW2 is done.
#      NOTE: This was later modified by Note #116 to move gas after Cyber,
#      then reverted by Note #122 to put gas back before Cyber.
#
#  85. PVZ ROACH DEFENSE: STALKER FIRST (NOT ZEALOT) WHEN ROACHES EXPECTED:
#      In the roach/bane defense production code, step 1 always produced a
#      zealot first for wall plug, regardless of whether roaches were coming.
#      When roach_bane_active was True (pool+gas timing detected at ~58s) but
#      the roach warren wasn't physically spotted until ~181s, the bot
#      produced zealot > sentry > stalker. Zealots are melee and get kited by
#      roaches — a stalker at the wall can shoot from range and is much more
#      effective vs roach aggression. FIX: When roach_bane_active OR
#      _pvz_roach_warren_seen is True (we know roaches are coming), step 1
#      produces a stalker instead of a zealot. The sentry gate condition was
#      updated to check for the first combat unit (stalker OR zealot) instead
#      of just zealot_count >= 1. The zealot fallback (step 4) now also uses
#      _know_roaches_coming = roach_bane_active OR _pvz_roach_warren_seen
#      instead of just _pvz_roach_warren_seen. Bane-only aggression (no
#      roach warren, no pool+gas timing) still produces zealot > sentry.
#
#  86. PVZ ROACH PUSH-OUT: When roaches/ravagers are within 10 of gatewall1
#      and NO zerglings are within 15, our entire army pushes OUT to engage
#      instead of holding position at the wall gap. Previously, units held
#      position at the wall gap even vs roaches, which was designed to block
#      zerglings from flooding through. But roaches are ranged (4 range) —
#      they don't flood through gaps. They sit at range and shell our buildings
#      while our army does nothing behind the wall. Stalkers outrange roaches
#      (6 vs 4) and should be pushing out to kite and kill them. FIX: Added
#      _roaches_near_wall_no_lings flag (computed at top of BASE DEFENSE
#      section in manage_army_movement). When True: (a) PART 1 wallgap hold
#      is skipped — no units get hold_position at the gap. (b) Plug zealot
#      and backup zealot are NOT held at the gap — they attack with the army.
#      (c) _enemy_near_wallgap is cleared — no wallgap traversal block.
#      (d) Threat detection extended to include roaches/ravagers within 10
#      of gatewall1 (they were previously outside the 8-from-ournat range).
#      (e) Plug/backup zealots are not excluded from attack orders. The
#      ling check range (15) is wider than the roach check range (10) because
#      lings can quickly reinforce and flood through the gap if we push out
#      carelessly — we need a safety margin. If lings appear, we revert to
#      standard wall-gap-hold behavior.
#
#  87. BUILDER PROBE DEFENSE: HELPER PROBE, CHAIN TAG SYNC & DEATH RECOVERY:
#      Three related fixes to prevent build stalls from enemy worker harass:
#      (a) HELPER PROBE (90% HP): When the builder probe drops below 90% HP
#          and is fighting an enemy worker, a second probe is pulled from the
#          mineral line to help kill the enemy worker faster. This prevents
#          the builder from getting worn down to 40% and needing a swap.
#          The helper is tracked in _builder_helper_tag and released when the
#          enemy worker leaves. Applies to ALL matchups (PvT SCV, PvZ drone,
#          PvP probe scout). Added to is_worker_locked(), get_free_worker()
#          exclusions, and away_tags for mining micro.
#      (b) CHAIN TAG SYNC: When the builder probe is replaced (40% HP, stuck,
#          or dead), all chain-specific probe tags (_pvt_gw_first_probe_tag,
#          _pvt_reaper_probe_tag, _pvp_probe_tag, _four_gate_probe_tag,
#          nexus_first_probe_tag) are updated to point to the new probe.
#          Without this sync, the chain state machine still references the
#          old/dead tag and can't reassign, causing the entire build to stall.
#          Implemented via _sync_chain_probe_tags(old_tag, new_tag) helper.
#      (c) GW_FIRST STATE 99 RECOVERY: When the builder probe dies while
#          building the gateway (killed by SCV scout), the gw_first state
#          machine goes to state 99 (inactive/handoff). But if the gateway
#          was never completed (build cancelled on probe death), the reaper
#          response state machine is stuck at state 0 waiting for a gateway
#          that will never appear. FIX: At state 99, if the gateway at
#          gatewall1 doesn't exist and isn't pending, reset to state 0 so
#          the gw_first state machine retries building the gateway.
# =========================================================================
#  88. GAS WORKERS NOT RETURNING TO GAS AFTER ENEMY WORKER RETALIATION:
#      When an enemy worker (SCV, drone, probe) attacks one of our gas
#      workers near our base, _worker_retaliation() makes the gas worker
#      fight back (attack order). After the fight ends (enemy dies or
#      leaves), the gas worker doesn't return to its assimilator — it sits
#      idle or gets reassigned to mine minerals by manage_complex_mining().
#      The worker's tag is still in self.gas_workers[assim_tag], so
#      manage_gas_workers() thinks the gas slot is full and doesn't assign
#      a replacement. Result: ghost gas slot (slot appears full but worker
#      mines minerals), gas income drops to zero.
#
#      Two specific failure modes:
#      (a) LEASH CANCEL: When a gas worker chases an enemy past 25 from
#          ourmain / 15 from ournat, the leash cancel code sent it to
#          mineral_field.closest_to() instead of back to its assimilator.
#      (b) POST-COMMIT DRIFT: After the enemy dies, _worker_combat_tags
#          is cleared next frame, and manage_complex_mining() treats the
#          gas worker as a regular mineral worker since it has no attack
#          order. The worker gets mineral mining orders, but its tag
#          remains in gas_workers — no replacement is ever assigned.
#
#      FIX: Added _retaliation_gas_pulled_tags tracking (mirrors the
#      _pvt_gas_pulled_tags pattern from _proxy_hidden_tech_worker_micro).
#      Gas workers that get pulled off to fight are tracked with their
#      assimilator tag. When the fight ends (enemy gone, worker outside
#      leash, or no longer in fighting_tags), the worker is explicitly
#      sent back to its assimilator (return cargo first if carrying).
#      The tracking dict is also added to manage_complex_mining's
#      excluded_tags and get_free_worker's exclusion list so mining micro
#      and builder systems don't interfere with the return-to-gas order.
# =========================================================================
#  92. GAS WORKERS NOT RETURNING TO GAS (GENERAL FIX):
#      In PvT matches (especially reaper_response build), probes that get
#      pulled from gas -- whether by worker retaliation, reaper micro, or
#      any other disruption -- often don't return to gas after the threat
#      clears. Their tag remains in self.gas_workers, so manage_gas_workers()
#      thinks the gas slot is full and never assigns a replacement. Result:
#      "ghost gas slot" (slot appears full but worker mines minerals or is
#      idle), gas income drops to near zero.
#
#      The existing DEV NOTE #88 fix only covers the retaliation scenario
#      (_retaliation_gas_pulled_tags), and the proxy hidden tech fix only
#      covers reapers in proxy games (_pvt_gas_pulled_tags). But in a
#      standard reaper_response game, neither mechanism covers all cases:
#      - A gas worker that retaliates against an enemy SCV and then gets
#        its return-to-gas order overridden by mining micro
#      - A gas worker that gets disrupted by any other mechanism
#
#      FIX: Added a gas worker integrity check at the start of
#      manage_gas_workers(). When no enemy units are within 25 of ourmain,
#      the check verifies that every worker in gas_workers is actually
#      gathering from its assigned assimilator (or returning cargo as part
#      of the normal gas cycle). If not, the worker is forced back to its
#      assimilator. Workers in active combat (_worker_combat_tags) or
#      tracked by existing return mechanisms (_retaliation_gas_pulled_tags,
#      _pvt_gas_pulled_tags, _pvt_fleeing_probe_tags) are skipped so their
#      attack/flee orders aren't overridden.
# =========================================================================
#  93. PVZ EARLY ROACH: STOP PROBES, SKIP TWILIGHT, 5 BATTERIES, IMMORTAL PUSH
#      In PvZ games when hallucination confirms a 1-base roach all-in (roach
#      warren + <25 enemy workers), the bot was making several critical mistakes:
#
#      (a) PROBE OVERPRODUCTION: _pvz_check_1base_roach() required 4+ stalkers
#          before blocking worker production. This meant probes kept being produced
#          for 60+ seconds after hallucination confirmed the all-in, wasting ~8
#          probes worth of resources on economy instead of army/batteries.
#          FIX: Removed stalker count requirement — block probes the moment roach
#          warren + low worker count is confirmed.
#
#      (b) TWILIGHT COUNCIL DELAY: The roach-bane state machine built twilight
#          (150/100 cost, 36s build time) before robos. For roach defense, we
#          don't need charge or blink — we need immortals and batteries NOW.
#          The 150/100 spent on twilight delays 2 robos + 1 immortal by ~45s.
#          FIX: Skip twilight entirely when _pvz_skip_twilight is set. Warpgate
#          research works from cyber core alone (SC2 only requires cyber, not
#          twilight — the bot's twilight gate was its own design choice).
#
#      (c) INSUFFICIENT BATTERIES: Only 2 batteries were built (states 85/85.6),
#          and the 2nd was often skipped due to no valid position found. The
#          battery search only looked in narrow bands behind the wallpylon.
#          FIX: Target 5 batteries minimum. Expanded the battery position search
#          to wider range (2.0-5.4 vs 2.0-3.4) and added a ring search around
#          the wallpylon. Lowered mineral threshold to 100 to start building
#          batteries faster.
#
#      (d) LATE IMMORTAL PRODUCTION: With only 1 robo, the first production was
#          an observer (30s), delaying the first immortal to 60+s after robo
#          completion. By then the defense had already collapsed.
#          FIX: Build 2 robos minimum for 1-base roach defense. One produces
#          observer, the other starts immortal immediately. Attack timing
#          triggers at 4 immortals instead of requiring charge (which needs
#          the twilight we skipped).
