#  91. PVP VOIDRAY MAIN PYLON BOUNCE FIX: In the PvP voidray build state
#      machine, state 6 (build main base pylon) had a bouncing bug where
#      the builder probe alternated between two positions every frame
#      indefinitely, never completing the pylon. In the observed game, the
#      probe bounced between (136, 132) and (133, 129) for 50+ seconds.
#
#      ROOT CAUSE: The code re-validated _pvp_pylon_pos with can_place()
#      every frame before issuing build(). When the builder probe stood on
#      the build site, can_place() returned False, so the cache was
#      invalidated (_pvp_pylon_pos = None). Next frame, a new position
#      was computed (often different), the probe walked there, can_place()
#      failed again (probe now on the new spot), cache invalidated again.
#      This created an infinite two-position bounce:
#        Frame A: cache pos A → can_place fails (probe on A) → invalidate
#        Frame B: re-compute → find pos B → can_place passes → build()
#        Frame C: cache pos B → can_place fails (probe on B) → invalidate
#        Frame D: re-compute → find pos A → can_place passes → build()
#        ... forever. The pylon is NEVER actually built.
#
#      SECONDARY ISSUE: manage_supply_midgame() had no exclusion for PvP
#      voidray. When supply_left < 6 (which is permanently true since the
#      pylon never completes), self.build(PYLON) could steal the builder
#      probe, further disrupting the build order.
#
#      FIX: Three changes:
#      (a) Lock the main pylon position once computed
#          (_pvp_main_pylon_locked_pos). This is never invalidated by
#          can_place() failures. Same pattern as Dev Note #26
#          (_4gate_main_pylon_in_transit) and PvZ's _main_pylon_target_pos.
#      (b) Once build() is issued, set _pvp_main_pylon_ordered and skip
#          can_place re-validation. Check for pylon completion via
#          structure existence instead. Only re-search on 5-second timeout.
#      (c) Added PvP voidray exclusion in manage_supply_midgame() for
#          states 5-7 (battery/pylon/stargate building), same as the
#          existing proxy hidden tech exclusion.
# =========================================================================
#  99. 4-IN-GAS BUG: UNTRACKED GAS WORKER WHEN gas_set >= 3 (Pass 0 fix):
#      When a state machine builder finishes an assimilator, SC2 auto-sends
#      the builder to mine from it. But manage_gas_workers() runs BEFORE the
#      state machine clears the gas builder tag (_pvt_reaper_gas1_tag etc.)
#      on the same frame. The builder is is_worker_locked() so Pass 0 skips
#      it. Pass 4 then assigns 3 workers to the "empty" gas_set. Next frame,
#      the builder tag is cleared, but gas_set already has 3 entries — the
#      old Pass 0 skipped (len >= 3). The untracked builder mines gas
#      alongside 3 tracked workers = 4 total.
#
#      This is especially triggered when the retaliation code (Dev Note #88)
#      pulls gas workers off to fight enemy workers. The retaliation
#      return-to-gas mechanism works correctly for TRACKED gas workers
#      (their tag stays in gas_workers, they get sent back after the fight).
#      But for UNTRACKED workers (gas builder auto-mining), the retaliation
#      code doesn't know they're gas workers (they're not in gas_workers),
#      so they don't get returned to gas after fighting — they drift to
#      minerals. Meanwhile, Pass 4 has filled the gas_set to 3. Next time
#      the untracked worker wanders near the assimilator (e.g. returning
#      cargo to the nearby nexus), SC2 auto-sends it back to the
#      assimilator it built, creating a 4th worker.
#
#      FIX: Extended Pass 0 to handle gas_set >= 3 cases. When an
#      untracked worker is found actively mining gas but gas_set is full:
#      (a) If any tracked worker in gas_set is NOT on the gas cycle
#          (wayward), replace it with the untracked worker.
#      (b) If all 3 tracked workers are on gas, replace the tracked worker
#          furthest from the assimilator with the untracked one (it's more
#      efficient to have a worker already at the assimilator than one
#      walking back).
#      Also added per-assimilator gas worker count logging in ECON status.
# =========================================================================
#  100. PASS 0 WAYWARD CHECK EXCLUDES RETALIATION/REAPER-TRACKED GAS WORKERS:
#      Pass 0 of manage_gas_workers() detects tracked workers that are NOT on
#      the gas cycle and marks them as "wayward" so they can be replaced by
#      untracked workers. But it didn't exclude workers tracked by
#      _retaliation_gas_pulled_tags or _pvt_gas_pulled_tags — these are gas
#      workers temporarily fighting enemy workers or fleeing reapers. They
#      WILL return to gas when the threat clears (their tag stays in
#      gas_workers and the return mechanism sends them back).
#
#      Without the exclusion, Pass 0 replaces the fighting gas worker with
#      an untracked worker. gas_set changes from {A, B, C} (A fighting) to
#      {D, B, C}. When the fight ends, the retaliation code sends A back to
#      gas (A is still in _retaliation_gas_pulled_tags), but A has been
#      removed from gas_workers. A becomes an untracked gas miner. Result:
#      3 tracked (D, B, C) + 1 untracked (A) = 4 physical gas miners.
#
#      FIX: Added exclusion checks for _retaliation_gas_pulled_tags,
#      _pvt_gas_pulled_tags, and _worker_combat_tags in Pass 0's wayward
#      worker detection. Fighting/fleeing gas workers are NOT wayward —
#      they're temporarily off gas and will return via their tracking
#      mechanism.
# =========================================================================
#  101. BUILDER PROBE DEFENSE CANCELS QUEUED PYLON BUILD (86 SUPPLY STUCK):
#      manage_builder_probe_defense() issues builder.attack(enemy) when an
#      enemy worker is in melee range. Before issuing the attack, it checks
#      the builder's FIRST order to avoid interrupting active BUILD commands.
#      But it only checked orders[0], not queued orders.
#
#      manage_flower_production() issues builder.move(pos) followed by
#      builder.build(PYLON, pos). The move() is the first order and the
#      build() is queued. When the builder defense code checked orders[0],
#      it saw MOVE (which is "safe to interrupt") and issued ATTACK. The
#      ATTACK cancelled both the MOVE and the queued BUILD. On the next
#      frame, is_builder_busy() saw ATTACK ability and cleared the pylon
#      task. manage_flower_production() then assigned a NEW pylon at a
#      DIFFERENT position (the previous position was reserved but cleared
#      when the builder became idle). This cycle repeats indefinitely —
#      every frame a new pylon position is chosen, the builder walks toward
#      it, then gets interrupted by builder defense, cancelling the pylon.
#      Result: stuck at ~86 supply, pylons never get built.
#
#      FIX: Scan ALL builder orders (not just the first) for build commands.
#      If any queued order is a build command (not in safe_to_interrupt),
#      skip the ATTACK entirely — the building placement is more important
#      than fighting a lone worker. Also added logging when skipping ATTACK
#      due to queued BUILD orders.
# =========================================================================
#  102. FORGE NEVER BUILDS — IS_BUILDER_BUSY / EXECUTE_WALL_OFF OSCILLATION:
#      On Torches AIE (and likely other maps), the forge at gatewall3 never
#      gets built. The builder probe arrives at the forge build position but
#      the build command is never issued. The probe sits idle indefinitely
#      while minerals pile up to 475+.
#
#      ROOT CAUSE: Two interacting bugs creating an infinite oscillation:
#
#      (a) execute_wall_off() checks probe.distance_to(forge_move_pos) > 1.0
#          to decide whether to pre-move or build. forge_move_pos is offset
#          1 unit towards ournat from forge_build_pos. The probe naturally
#          stops at forge_build_pos due to SC2 pathing, which is exactly
#          1.0 from forge_move_pos. Due to floating point, the distance is
#          just barely > 1.0 (e.g., 1.004), so the pre-move branch fires
#          every frame instead of the build branch. The probe is told to
#          move to forge_move_pos, but it's already there (effectively).
#
#      (b) is_builder_busy() (forge task, line 9341-9345) immediately
#          clears the builder task when the probe is idle at the target
#          with no forge building. This was designed as a safety check for
#          failed builds, but the build was NEVER attempted — it was blocked
#          by is_builder_busy() itself during the entire travel phase. When
#          the probe arrives and is_builder_busy() clears the task on the
#          first idle frame, execute_wall_off() re-assigns the task on the
#          next frame, then is_builder_busy() clears it again — infinite
#          oscillation.
#
#      The oscillation cycle:
#        Frame A: is_builder_busy() sees idle probe → clears task → False
#        Frame B: execute_wall_off() runs, dist>1.0 → pre-move, task="forge"
#        Frame C: is_builder_busy() sees idle probe → clears task → False
#        ... forever. The "AT POSITION" debug log never appears because
#        the code never reaches the build section.
#
#      FIX: Two changes:
#      (a) execute_wall_off(): Check distance to forge_BUILD_pos with
#          threshold 2.0 (not forge_move_pos with 1.0). A probe within
#          2.0 of the build position can issue probe.build(FORGE, pos).
#          Also reset _forge_idle_retries when the forge task is assigned.
#      (b) is_builder_busy() forge task: Add _forge_idle_retries counter.
#          On the first idle frame (retry==1), return False to let
#          execute_wall_off() run and issue the build. On subsequent idle
#          frames, return True (busy) to protect the task. If 30 frames
#          pass with no build, clear the task as genuinely failed.
#          Counter is reset on probe replacement and task reassignment.
# =========================================================================
#  103. LATE-GAME ATTACK STUCK AT ENEMYNAT WITH OVERLORDS:
#      After killing most enemy bases in PvZ, the army repeatedly attack-
#      moves to enemynat instead of hunting visible enemy buildings.
#      Overlords at enemynat cause immortals (which cannot attack air)
#      to get stuck trying to engage targets they can't hit.
#
#      ROOT CAUSES (three interacting bugs):
#
#      (a) _pvt_main_destroyed never triggers in PvZ. The flag only
#          activates when a unit reaches within 2 of enemymain (line
#          13591). In PvZ, the army may kill nat/3rd/4th bases without
#          ever reaching the main position, so the hunt-visible-
#          structures code at line 14284 never activates. The army
#          follows the waypoint sweep, attack-moving to positions
#          instead of targeting buildings directly.
#
#      (b) is_ground_only classification was wrong (line 14331). It
#          listed {ZEALOT, SENTRY} but Sentries CAN attack air (their
#          ranged weapon hits both ground and air). Immortals and Adepts
#          CANNOT attack air (ground-only weapons) but were missing.
#          This caused the per-unit structure targeting code to send
#          immortals after flying buildings, and to NOT send sentries
#          after flying buildings when they should.
#
#      (c) _SWEEP_CAN_ATTACK_AIR / _CAN_ATTACK_AIR sets included
#          IMMORTAL and HIGHTEMPLAR. Neither can attack air. This
#          caused the sweep micro to think immortals could engage
#          flying enemies, leading to them "having enemies in range"
#          (overlords) and not retreating to find ground targets.
#
#      FIXES:
#      (a) Added townhall count trigger for _pvt_main_destroyed: when
#          enemy has 0-1 townhalls remaining, activate hunt mode
#          regardless of army position. This works for all matchups.
#      (b) Fixed is_ground_only = {ZEALOT, IMMORTAL, ADEPT}. Sentries
#          are now correctly classified as anti-air units.
#      (c) Removed IMMORTAL and HIGHTEMPLAR from _SWEEP_CAN_ATTACK_AIR
#          and _CAN_ATTACK_AIR. Now = {STALKER, SENTRY, VOIDRAY,
#          COLOSSUS}.
#      (d) Upgraded the "not valid_targets" fallback (line 14265) from
#          simple army-center targeting to per-unit targeting with
#          ground-only unit awareness. Also added fog-of-war ground
#          structure check before falling back to blind enemynat
#          attack-move.
# =========================================================================
#  104. PYLON BUILD CANCELLED BY STATE MACHINE + STUCK COUNTER FALSE POSITIVE:
#      In the PvT reaper response, the builder probe's pylon build order
#      gets cancelled on the next frame by two interacting bugs:
#
#      (a) STATE 5 "nearby_pylons" CHECK DOESN'T REQUIRE .ready:
#          self.structures(UnitTypeId.PYLON).closer_than(7, pylon_pos)
#          includes UNDER-CONSTRUCTION pylons (is_ready=False). On the
#          very next frame after probe.build(PYLON, pos) is issued, the
#          pylon appears in self.structures. The "nearby_pylons" check
#          finds it and advances the state machine to state 6 (GW2).
#          State 6 then issues probe.move(gw2_pos), which CANCELS
#          the pylon build order. The pylon is never constructed.
#          FIX: Changed to .ready.closer_than(7, pylon_pos) — only
#          completed pylons advance the state.
#
#      (b) ALREADY_PENDING PRE-MOVE CANCELS ACTIVE BUILD:
#          The old code at state 5 checked already_pending(PYLON) > 0
#          and issued probe.move(battery_premove) "to save time" while
#          waiting for the pylon to complete. But move() cancels the
#          active PROTOSSBUILD_PYLON order! This is the same class of
#          bug as Dev Note #26 (never issue move() to a probe that has
#          an active build order).
#          FIX: Removed the pre-move entirely. Just wait for the pylon
#          to complete — don't move the probe.
#
#      (c) _check_building_at_target MISSING TASK NAMES:
#          The stuck counter in is_builder_busy() uses
#          _check_building_at_target() to verify whether a building
#          exists at the builder's target. Missing task names caused
#          it to return False for actively-building probes:
#          - "nexus" (reaper state 2) was missing — only "3rd_nexus"
#            was handled. This caused the nexus builder to be declared
#            "stuck" after 11 steps, clearing the task and blacklisting
#            valid positions.
#          - "cyber"/"pre_cyber" (reaper state 3) were missing.
#          - "gateway2", "ramp_temp_pylon", "four_gate_chain" were missing.
#          FIX: Added all missing task names to _check_building_at_target.
# =========================================================================
#  105. 4-IN-GAS BUG: TAG REMOVED WHEN WORKER TEMPORARILY NOT FOUND:
#      Multiple places in manage_gas_workers() used self.workers.find_by_tag()
#      to check if a gas worker is still alive. If find_by_tag returns None,
#      the tag was immediately removed from gas_set, freeing a slot for Pass 4
#      to assign a new worker. But find_by_tag can return None for LIVING
#      workers that are temporarily invisible to the unit query:
#
#      (a) INSIDE ASSIMILATOR: While a probe is inside the assimilator
#          harvesting gas, the SC2 API may not return it in the workers
#          collection, making find_by_tag return None.
#      (b) RETALIATION RETURN DELAY: When _worker_retaliation() sends a
#          fighting gas worker back to the assimilator, it clears
#          _retaliation_gas_pulled_tags on the same frame. If the worker
#          is still walking back (MOVE/HARVEST_GATHER order not yet
#          processed by the game engine), and happens to be inside the
#          assimilator for one frame, Pass 1 removes the tag. Pass 4
#          assigns a replacement. When the original worker exits the
#          assimilator, it resumes mining gas untracked = 4 workers.
#      (c) PASS 0 WAYWARD CHECK: When gas_set >= 3 and an untracked
#          worker was found near the assimilator, Pass 0 checked each
#          tracked worker. If find_by_tag returned None, the tag was
#          marked as "wayward" and replaced with the untracked worker.
#      (d) PASS 3 INTEGRITY CHECK: If find_by_tag returned None for a
#          gas worker, the tag was immediately discarded from gas_set.
#
#      FIX: Three changes to implement tag-based gas worker tracking:
#      1. Pass 1: Replaced immediate tag removal with a grace-period
#         approach using _gas_worker_missing_frames dict. Tags are only
#         removed after 45 consecutive missing frames (~2.8s at 16fps).
#         When a tag IS found, the counter resets to 0.
#      2. Pass 0: Don't mark a tag as "wayward" when find_by_tag
#         returns None — skip it instead. Pass 1 handles confirmed
#         dead workers via the grace period.
#      3. Pass 3: Don't discard a tag when find_by_tag returns None —
#         just skip it. Pass 1 handles confirmed dead workers.
#      Also added a POST-CHECK that detects 4-in-gas by comparing
#      tracked gas_set counts to physical worker counts near each
#      assimilator, with detailed logging for debugging.
# =========================================================================
#  106. PYLON BUILD STUCK AT 78/86 SUPPLY (move+build WITHOUT queue=True):
#      manage_flower_production() issued builder.move(target_pos) followed
#      by builder.build(PYLON, target_pos) in the same frame without
#      queue=True. In the burnysc2 framework, calling build() after move()
#      without queue=True causes the build() to REPLACE the move() order.
#      If the probe is too far from the position, build() fails (returns
#      False), and both commands are lost — the probe ends up with NO
#      orders at all. On the next frame:
#      (a) If the probe was still far from the target, is_builder_busy()
#          sees it idle, clears the pylon task, and manage_flower_production
#          assigns a DIFFERENT pylon position (the first position's
#          flower_reserved slot was set, so the offset loop skips it).
#      (b) If the probe happened to be close to the target but build()
#          still failed (e.g., position temporarily blocked), the stuck
#          counter increments and eventually blacklists the position.
#      This cycle repeats indefinitely — different pylon position each
#      time, probe walks toward it, build fails, task cleared, new
#      position assigned, probe redirected. No pylons ever get built.
#      Meanwhile, supply stays stuck at 78-86, and eventually the probe
#      gets pulled back to mining when all positions are blacklisted.
#
#      The emergency supply pylon code had the same issue: it issued
#      emerg_worker.move() without tracking the worker, so
#      manage_complex_mining() pulled it back to mining on the next frame.
#
#      FIX: Three changes:
#      1. Main pylon code: Changed to conditional pattern (same as
#         stargate pre-move code): if far, only move(); if close, only
#         build(). The builder_current_task is set BEFORE the command so
#         is_builder_busy() protects the probe while it walks. When the
#         probe arrives at the target and becomes idle, is_builder_busy()
#         re-issues build(PYLON, target) with retry logic.
#      2. Emergency pylon code: Same conditional pattern. Added
#         _emergency_pylon_worker_tag and _emergency_pylon_target
#         tracking so the worker is excluded from manage_complex_mining()
#         via away_tags. Added follow-up logic at the start of
#         manage_flower_production() to issue the build command when the
#         emergency worker arrives at the target.
#      3. Added _emergency_pylon_worker_tag to manage_complex_mining()
#         away_tags and excluded_tags so the emergency pylon worker
#         doesn't get pulled back to mining while walking to the pylon.
#  107. GATEWAY2 BUILDER PROBE IDLES WITH MINERALS THEN RETREATS TO MINING:
#      In PvT reaper response, the probe assigned to build gateway 2 (gw2)
#      would sit idle at the build location with 150+ minerals available
#      instead of building, then return to mining when the enemy reaper
#      entered the base. Three interacting root causes:
#
#      (a) IS_BUILDER_BUSY "gateway2" HANDLER HAS NO RETRY MECHANISM:
#          When the probe was idle at the target (build() failed silently
#          due to position temporarily blocked by a unit, can_place race
#          condition, etc.), the handler immediately cleared the gateway2
#          task with zero retries. By contrast, the "pylon" handler has
#          10 retries (~5 seconds) and re-issues build() on each retry.
#          FIX: Added full retry mechanism matching the pylon pattern:
#          - Only increment stuck counter when can_afford(GATEWAY)
#          - Re-issue builder.build(GATEWAY, target_pos) on each retry
#          - Blacklist position after 10 retries exhausted
#          - Reset stuck counter on success or when traveling
#
#      (b) REAPER STATE 7 ADVANCES TO STATE 8 BEFORE GW2 IS CONFIRMED:
#          State 7 issued probe.build(GATEWAY, gw2_pos) and immediately
#          advanced to state 8 (forge). If the build() call failed
#          silently, the state machine had already moved past the GW2
#          build state and would never retry. Additionally, state 8
#          would overwrite builder_current_task from "gateway2" to
#          "forge" and issue probe.move(forge_pos), cancelling any
#          pending gateway build order.
#          FIX: State 6 no longer advances to state 7 when the build
#          command is issued. Instead, it waits for the gateway to
#          appear in self.structures() (confirmed at the top of the
#          state handler). If builder_current_task is "gateway2", state 6
#          just returns and lets is_builder_busy() handle retries. State 8
#          now checks for an active "gateway2" task before starting forge.
#
#      (c) STATE 8 OVERWRITES "gateway2" TASK WITH "forge":
#          When state 8 runs (forge), it unconditionally sets
#          builder_current_task = "forge" and builder_task_target = forge_pos,
#          overwriting any active gateway2 task. If the gateway build
#          command hadn't been processed by the SC2 engine yet (1-frame
#          race condition), probe.move(forge_pos) would cancel it.
#          FIX: State 8 now returns early if builder_current_task is
#          "gateway2", waiting for the gateway task to complete first.
#  108. STALKER REAPER DEFENSE USES WRONG MINERAL LINE:
#      When a reaper harasses the natural base, the stalker intercept
#      position was always calculated using the MAIN base mineral center.
#      The stalker would position itself between the reaper and the wrong
#      mineral line, leaving the natural workers unprotected while the
#      stalker guarded the main mineral line from a reaper that wasn't
#      even there. Same issue in the builder probe reaper flee logic.
#      FIX: All three reaper defense code paths now check which base
#      (main or natural) is closest to the reaper and use THAT base's
#      mineral center for the intercept/flee position:
#      (a) _proxy_hidden_tech_stalker_micro: post-sweep defender and
#          pre-sweep intercept stalker
#      (b) manage_pvt_stalker_defense: midgame reaper intercept
#      (c) manage_builder_probe_defense: reaper flee position
#  109. STALKER ATTACK CANCELLED BY wall_rally MOVE + ENEMY RAMP LOG SPAM:
#      (a) STALKER ATTACK RESET: In _proxy_hidden_tech_stalker_micro, the
#          wall_rally fallback code at the bottom of the per-stalker loop
#          issued stalker.move(wall_rally) unconditionally, even when the
#          stalker had an active ATTACK order on a nearby enemy. This caused
#          every ATTACK command to be cancelled on the next frame, resulting
#          in attacks_issued=16 attacks_landed=0 (stalkers could never shoot).
#          The same issue existed in the structure-chase move() call.
#          FIX: Added ATTACK-order guard before both move() calls. If the
#          stalker's top order is ATTACK/ATTACK_ATTACK, skip the move().
#      (b) ENEMY RAMP LOG SPAM: _get_enemy_ramp_position() was called from
#          per-stalker loops (5+ calls per stalker per frame), recalculating
#          and logging every time. With 10 stalkers at 16fps, this produced
#          480+ identical log messages per second and wasted CPU.
#          FIX: Cache the result in _cached_enemy_ramp_position and throttle
#          the debug log to once every 30 seconds.
# =========================================================================
#  107. PVZ ROACH WARREN: CHARGE BEFORE ROBO UNITS (OBSERVER/IMMORTAL):
#      When a roach warren was spotted in PvZ, manage_pvz_roach_response()
#      immediately produced an observer (75 gas) and then immortals (100 gas
#      each) from the robo. With only 2 gas geysers (~200 gas/min), the bot
#      never accumulated 100 gas — every time gas got close to 100, an
#      immortal or observer consumed it. The charge research code in
#      manage_production_buildings() checked can_afford(UpgradeId.CHARGE)
#      which required 100 minerals + 100 gas. Since the robo production ran
#      FIRST (Developer Note #83: robo before warpgates), and the army
#      production also spent gas on stalkers (50 gas each), charge was NEVER
#      researched in entire games. Result: zealots without charge are melee
#      and get kited to death by roaches — they're useless. The entire army
#      was just stalkers + immortals with no chargelot backbone.
#      FIX: Added Priority 0 in manage_pvz_roach_response() that researches
#      charge BEFORE any robo production. When twilight is ready and charge
#      hasn't started, robo production is completely blocked (robos stay
#      idle) so gas accumulates for charge. Also added gas reservation in
#      both the roach defense stalker production and SEQUENCE 4 warpgate
#      production: when twilight is ready but charge hasn't started, 100 gas
#      is reserved for charge, preventing stalkers from draining it. After
#      charge starts researching, robo production resumes normally with
#      observer first, then immortals.
# =========================================================================
#  108. PYLON POSITION BOUNCING AT 86 SUPPLY: The builder probe kept
#      moving between different pylon positions instead of building at
#      one position. Root cause: Something (likely a state machine or
#      defense code) was issuing MOVE commands to the builder probe
#      that redirected it away from the pylon target (e.g., MOVE to
#      gw1 position instead of pylon target). When the builder became
#      idle far from the pylon target, is_builder_busy() cleared the
#      pylon task. manage_flower_production then picked a NEW pylon
#      position (different from the previous one because flower_reserved
#      was cleared), and the cycle repeated indefinitely.
#      FIX: Three changes:
#      (a) STICKY PYLON POSITION: Once a pylon position is chosen, it's
#          saved in _sticky_pylon_target. If the pylon task is cleared
#          and needs reassignment, the same position is reused (if still
#          valid and not blacklisted) instead of picking a new one.
#      (b) RE-ISSUE MOVE ON HIJACK: In is_builder_busy(), when the
#          builder has MOVE to wrong position with pylon task, re-issue
#          the move command to the correct pylon target instead of just
#          logging an error. This immediately corrects the probe's path
#          instead of waiting for the task to be cleared and reassigned.
#      (c) Added debug counters for pylon redirects and sticky position
#          usage, logged in the periodic SUMMARY output.
# =========================================================================
#  109. PVZ CHARGE BEFORE ROBO UNITS EVEN WHEN TWILIGHT IS PENDING:
#      The _charge_gate_passed flag in manage_pvz_roach_response()
#      included `not twilights_roach` which evaluates to True when
#      twilight is under construction (not ready yet). This allowed
#      robo production (observer/immortals) to proceed before charge
#      was researched, because the code treated "twilight not ready"
#      the same as "twilight doesn't exist". With twilight pending,
#      the robo produced an observer (75 gas) and immortals (100 gas
#      each), draining gas so charge (100 gas) was never affordable.
#      FIX: Changed _charge_gate_passed to only allow robo production
#      before charge when twilight truly doesn't exist (not built AND
#      not pending). If twilight is pending (under construction), robo
#      production is blocked until twilight completes and charge starts.
#      Also extended the gas reservation in SEQUENCE 4 (manage_army_production)
#      to reserve 100 gas for charge when twilight is pending (not just
#      when ready), preventing stalkers from draining gas meant for charge.
# =========================================================================
#  110. PVZ MUTALISK ZONAL DEFENSE: Stalkers sat at the wall pylon doing
#      nothing while mutalisks killed workers at our main and natural bases.
#      ROOT CAUSE: The PvZ base defense in manage_army_movement had TWO
#      critical problems:
#      (a) Only 8-range detection from ourmain/ournat — mutas attack from
#          outside this range while stalkers at the wall pylon are ~25 from
#          ourmain, so they never "see" the threat.
#      (b) Flying units explicitly filtered out with `not getattr(e,
#          'is_flying', False)` — mutalisks are NEVER detected as threats
#          by the ground defense, regardless of range.
#      Additionally, no PvZ zonal defense existed (PvT and PvP both have
#      25-from-main zonal defense, but PvZ had nothing for air).
#      FIX: Added PvZ air zonal defense section in manage_army_movement
#      that detects mutalisks/corruptors/broodlords within 25 of ourmain
#      and 15 of ournat. Stalkers and sentries attack the closest air
#      threat. Zealots are excluded (can't attack air). Stalker kiting
#      vs mutas: on weapon cooldown, move toward wall pylon for shield
#      recharge; off cooldown, attack. The army positioning section (which
#      sends stalkers to main_army_pos near wall pylon) is updated to
#      skip units that are actively attacking air threats, preventing
#      the move-to-wall-pylon order from overriding air defense orders.
#      Also added _pvz_air_threat_detected flag (set when spire seen or
#      mutalisks spotted) and _pvz_muta_tags_near_base (per-frame set
#      of muta tags in zonal range).
# =========================================================================
#  111. PVZ SENTRY ENERGY RECHARGE: Sentry spawned at 3:04 but didn't
#      receive energy recharge until 3:23 (~19 second delay). The sentry
#      spawns with 50 energy and needs 75 for hallucination phoenix scout
#      (or 100 for guardian shield). Without energy recharge, it takes
#      ~32 seconds for natural energy regen (0.7875 energy/sec) to reach
#      75 energy from 50.
#      ROOT CAUSE: PvT has a dedicated "Energy Recharge sentry" in its
#      chrono priority system (Priority 4), but PvZ had NO equivalent.
#      The only sentry energy recharge in PvZ was in manage_sentry_scout
#      which only fires when scout_attempted is False — once the first
#      hallucination is cast, the sentry is on its own for energy. This
#      meant the sentry waited for natural regen instead of getting an
#      instant energy boost from a nexus.
#      FIX: Added _pvz_sentry_energy_charged flag and energy recharge
#      logic to both the gateway_first/emergency chrono section and the
#      nexus_first step 3 chrono section. The sentry gets an instant
#      ENERGYRECHARGE from the highest-energy nexus as soon as it spawns,
#      cutting the wait from ~19 seconds to ~0.5 seconds.
# =========================================================================
#  112. PVT BUILDER PROBE BOUNCING BETWEEN GW1 AND NEXUS: After the
#      gateway_first state machine builds the gateway and hands off the
#      builder probe to the reaper response state machine, the probe
#      bounced between gw1 (111.5, 38.5) and the nexus (117.5, 28.5)
#      for 25+ seconds instead of moving to the nexus to prepare for
#      building it.
#      ROOT CAUSE: Two systems fought over the probe every frame:
#      (a) The reaper state machine (state 1: "build nexus") couldn't
#          afford the nexus yet (400 minerals), so it pre-moved the
#          probe toward the natural nexus position.
#      (b) is_builder_busy() detected the probe had a MOVE to the
#          nexus but builder_task_target was still (111.5, 38.5) from
#          the stale "gateway" task. It re-issued move(gw1_pos),
#          pulling the probe back toward the gateway.
#      The gateway was already under construction (the build command
#      was issued), so the "gateway" task was stale. But is_builder_busy()
#      never reached its "check if building exists at target" code
#      because it was intercepted by the "builder has MOVE to wrong
#      position" handler first, which re-issued the move every frame.
#      FIX (two-part):
#      (a) is_builder_busy() MOVE mismatch handler: Before re-issuing
#          move to the task target, check if the building at the task
#          target already exists (started or complete). If it does,
#          the task is stale — clear it and return False, letting the
#          probe proceed to its new destination. This prevents the
#          bounce for ANY stale task, not just gateway.
#      (b) Reaper state machine state 1: Explicitly clear the stale
#          "gateway" builder task when entering state 1. Check if the
#          gateway exists or is pending at the task target, and if so,
#          clear builder_current_task and builder_task_target. This
#          is a defensive fix that prevents the mismatch from ever
#          occurring.
# =========================================================================
#  113. PVT PROXY MARAUDER BUILD ORDER CHANGE + BUILDER STUCK FALSE POSITIVE:
#      Two changes to the gateway_first state machine for proxy marauder:
#      (a) BUILD ORDER CHANGE: The old proxy marauder order was
#          GW1 → Gas1 → Gas2 → Core, which delayed stalker production
#          because we only had 1 gateway while building gas. The new order
#          is GW1 → GW2 → Gas1 → Gas2 → Core → Main Pylon → Supply Pylon.
#          GW2 is built right after GW1 for faster stalker output.
#          Added state 2 (build GW2) and state 2.1 (wait for GW2) for
#          proxy marauder, and states 6 (main pylon) and 7 (supply pylon)
#          after cyber core. State 8 is the "done" transition for proxy
#          marauder (state 6 remains "done" for standard).
#      (b) BUILDER STUCK FALSE POSITIVE: In state 4, when the gateway
#          was below 80% progress, the code set builder_current_task=
#          "pre_cyber" with builder_task_target=gw.position. The probe
#          was correctly waiting at the gateway, but is_builder_busy()
#          saw the probe AT the task target with a "pre_cyber" task and
#          no cyber core being built — triggering the stuck counter.
#          After 7 seconds of "stuck", the probe was replaced with a
#          new one from the mineral line, wasting 10+ seconds of walk
#          time. FIX: Changed the waiting phase to use task "gateway_wait"
#          with builder_task_target=None. This clearly indicates the probe
#          is intentionally waiting (not lost or stuck). Added handler
#          in is_builder_busy() that returns True as long as a gateway
#          is still under construction, and clears the task once it's done.
#
#  114. PVZ SCOUT LOW-HP RETREAT + BUILD ORDER TRIGGER:
#      In PvZ, the scout probe was dying to enemy worker chases without
#      triggering a build order. Two problems: (a) the only HP check was
#      inside State 1 of the scout FSM (health-only at 50%), meaning a
#      probe being chased during its initial queued path had no HP check
#      at all, and (b) choose_opening_build() for PvZ required
#      enemy_main_minerals_complete=True (all 8 mineral fields seen), so
#      if the scout died before completing the mineral scan, no build
#      order was ever chosen. FIX: Three-part change:
#      (a) UNIVERSAL 60% TOTAL HP CHECK: Added at the TOP of
#          manage_scout_probe_timing(), BEFORE any state-specific logic.
#          When (health+shield)/(health_max+shield_max) < 0.60, the scout
#          immediately gathers the closest mineral field to OURMAIN (not
#          itself), sets _pvz_scout_returned_early=True, and clears
#          opening_probe_scout. This catches probes being chased during
#          their initial queued path as well as in any FSM state.
#      (b) BUILD ORDER TRIGGER: choose_opening_build() PvZ section now
#          triggers when EITHER enemy_main_minerals_complete=True (scout
#          saw full mineral line) OR _pvz_scout_returned_early=True
#          (scout retreated/died). Also added forge_first for very low
#          worker counts: <=4 workers = forge_first (early pool/cheese),
#          <=15 = gateway_first, >15 = nexus_first.
#      (c) STATE 1 STALE CHECK REMOVED: The old 50% health-only check
#          in State 1 (wrap after pool finish) was replaced by the
#          universal 60% total HP check that runs first in all states.
#
#  115. ROACH/BANE GAS NEVER BUILT WHEN GW2 ALREADY EXISTS:
#      When the roach/bane state machine enters state 80 or 81 and GW2
#      already exists (built by the gateway_first path before the redirect
#      from nexus_first), both states skip directly to state 82, BYPASSING
#      state 81.5. State 81.5 is where _rb_gas_triggered = True gets set.
#      Without this flag, _roach_bane_build_gas_parallel() is never called
#      and gas is NEVER built. The bot ends up with 0 gas the entire game.
#      FIX: Both state 80 and state 81 now set _rb_gas_triggered = True
#      before skipping to state 82 when GW2 already exists. Also fixed
#      the status display (rb_state → nf_state) which was always showing
#      "ROACH-BANE(state=0)" because rb_state was never assigned.
#
#  116. ROACH/BANE BUILD ORDER: CYBER BEFORE GAS:
#      Changed the roach/bane build order from:
#        Nexus > GW1 > GW2 > Gas1 > Gas2 > Cyber > Main Pylon > ...
#      To:
#        Nexus > GW1 > GW2 > Cyber > Gas1 > Gas2 > Main Pylon(after sentry) > ...
#      Getting cyber out first means we can start warpgate research and
#      produce sentries/stalkers sooner. Gas is triggered in state 83.5
#      (after Cyber is verified) instead of state 81.5 (after GW2).
#      Main pylon still waits for sentry/stalker production to start.
#      Also removed the gas trigger from states 80/81/81.5 (moved to 83.5),
#      and added it to state 82's cyber-skip bypass path.
#      **SUPERSEDED by Note #122**: Gas before cyber is better against roach
#      rushes because gas income is already flowing when cyber finishes,
#      allowing immediate stalker production. Reverted to GW2 > Gas1 > Gas2 > Cyber.
#
#  117. PVP CANNON RUSH: PROBE CHASE BOUNCING (sticky chaser):
#      When an enemy probe went briefly out of vision, the old code
#      immediately cleared BOTH _pvp_suspicious_probe_tag AND
#      _pvp_suspicious_chaser_tag. This released the chaser probe back
#      to mining. When the enemy probe reappeared a frame later, a
#      DIFFERENT chaser was assigned, causing the "probe attacks once
#      then returns to mining, different probe comes out" behavior.
#      FIX: (a) When enemy probe goes out of vision, keep the chaser
#      and move it toward the last known position for up to 3 seconds.
#      If the probe reappears, the same chaser continues. (b) Added
#      _pvp_suspicious_chaser_tag and _pvp_chase_probe_tags to
#      _worker_retaliation's excluded_tags, preventing retaliation
#      from canceling chase attack orders and sending chasers to mine.
#      (c) Added _pvp_suspicious_probe_last_pos and
#      _pvp_suspicious_probe_lost_time for position tracking.
#
#  118. PVP CANNON RUSH: PULL 4 PROBES PER BUILDING CANNON:
#      When enemy photon cannons started building near our base, the
#      bot had NO logic to pull probes to attack them. It only chased
#      enemy probes and sent zealots to kill cannons. The critical
#      window where 4 probes can kill a building cannon before it
#      completes was completely missed. FIX: Added new function
#      _pvp_cannon_rush_probe_fight_cannons() that:
#      (a) Finds building enemy cannons within 20 of ourmain / 15 of ournat
#      (b) Assigns 4 mining probes per building cannon to attack it
#      (c) Releases probes when cannons complete (retreats to mine)
#      (d) Retreats probes with HP < 60%
#      (e) Added _pvp_cannon_fighter_tags to is_worker_locked, away_tags,
#          get_free_worker, and _worker_retaliation exclusions
#      Also updated status display to show chase/fighter counts.
#
#  119. PVP CANNON RUSH: PROBE CHASE ROTATION (chaser transfer bug):
#      When cannon rush was confirmed (_pvp_cannon_rush=True), the transfer
#      code in _pvp_early_probe_detect() checked is_worker_locked(chaser)
#      WITHOUT temporarily clearing _pvp_suspicious_chaser_tag first. Since
#      is_worker_locked includes the chaser's own tag, it always returned
#      True, preventing the transfer to _pvp_chase_probe_tags. The chaser
#      was released (_pvp_suspicious_chaser_tag=None) without being added
#      to the chase system, so a DIFFERENT probe got assigned next frame.
#      This caused "probe attacks once then returns to mining, different
#      probe comes out" rotation behavior.
#      FIX: (a) Temporarily clear _pvp_suspicious_chaser_tag before
#      checking is_worker_locked in the transfer code (same pattern as
#      lines 8677-8683). (b) Added defense-in-depth guard in
#      issue_mining_command() to never issue mining commands to
#      chase/fighter probes, even if they somehow end up eligible.
#
#  120. PVP CANNON RUSH: STRUCTURE-BASED DETECTION:
#      The cannon rush defense (probe fight, chase, zealot micro) only
#      activated when _pvp_cannon_rush=True, which was only set by
#      detect_enemy_forge() seeing the enemy forge. But in many cannon
#      rushes, the forge is built in the enemy's own base (outside our
#      vision). Their probe builds pylons and cannons inside our base
#      before we ever see the forge. Result: no probes pulled to fight
#      building cannons because _pvp_cannon_rush was still False.
#      FIX: Added _pvp_detect_cannon_rush_from_structures() that detects
#      cannon rush from enemy PHOTONCANNON within 20/15 of ourmain/ournat
#      or enemy PYLON within 15/12 of ourmain/ournat. Sets _pvp_cannon_rush
#      = True and overrides opening to forge_first, same as forge detection.
#      Runs after detect_enemy_forge() in on_step so forge detection has
#      priority but structure detection catches the missed cases.
#
#  121. 3RD BASE DEFENSE: Army never defends 3rd base (our3rd):
#      The base defense threat detection in manage_army_movement() only
#      checked within 8 of ourmain and 8 of ournat. The 3rd base is
#      typically 25+ distance from ournat, so enemies attacking it were
#      never detected as threats. The army sat idle at the wall while
#      roaches/lings destroyed the 3rd nexus.
#      FIX: Added enemies_near_3rd detection (within 15 of our3rd) to
#      the base defense threat scan. Only scans when our3rd is set and
