#  122. PVZ ROACH-BANE BUILD ORDER: GAS BEFORE CYBER (REVERT OF NOTE #116):
#      Reverted the roach/bane build order from:
#        Nexus > GW1 > GW2 > Cyber > Gas1 > Gas2 > Main Pylon > ...
#      Back to:
#        Nexus > GW1 > GW2 > Gas1 > Gas2 > Cyber > Main Pylon(after sentry) > ...
#      ROOT CAUSE: In a PvZ roach rush game, the Cyber-before-Gas order (Note #116)
#      caused us to have 0 gas income when the cyber core finished at ~115s. The
#      first stalker couldn't be produced until ~169s because gas1+2 only started
#      building AFTER cyber was verified, and then we had to wait for gas to mine
#      enough for a stalker (50 gas). By that time, roaches were already attacking.
#      With Gas-before-Cyber, gas1 starts building at ~80s (after GW2 verified in
#      state 81.5) and gas2 starts after gas1 completes (~95s). By the time cyber
#      finishes (~120s), we have 2 geysers mining and enough gas for immediate
#      stalker production. The ~50s head start on gas income is critical against
#      roach timing attacks where every second counts.
#      FIX: Moved _rb_gas_triggered from state 83.5 (after Cyber) back to state
#      81.5 (after GW2). Also added gas triggers to states 80 (GW2 already exists)
#      and 81 (GW2 exists during build step) as safety checks. State 83.5 keeps
#      a fallback trigger but it should never fire in normal flow.
#      Supersedes Note #116.
#  124. 3RD NEXUS UNDER ATTACK — SKIP PYLON/BATTERY + 2-BASE TIMING:
#      When zerglings attack our 3rd nexus, the pylon builder gets killed
#      and the state machine gets stuck at state 67.5 waiting for a pylon
#      that will never be built. Additionally, investing 175 minerals
#      (pylon + battery) into a base under attack is wasteful.
#      FIX: Added _check_3rd_nexus_under_attack() detection that checks
#      for zerglings within 15 of our3rd. When ANY zerglings are near
#      the 3rd nexus, _3rd_nexus_under_attack is set, causing states
#      66.8/67/67.5/67.6 to skip the pylon and battery at the 3rd base.
#      When 6+ zerglings are near our3rd, we cancel the nexus (if still
#      building) or rally it to ournat (if complete), then transition
#      to the 2-base twilight/forge/chargelot/warpgate timing attack by
#      setting hatch_first_lings_spotted = True. If the nexus is already
#      complete, it rallies probes to ournat and continues production,
#      while the 3rd base pylon/battery are skipped to avoid stalling.
#      The _pvz_2base_timing_attack flag gates the aggressive production
#      mode (mass chargelots from warpgates + charge research priority).
#
#  123. FLOWER PRODUCTION PYLON BOUNCE FIX: The builder probe would
#      bounce between different pylon positions indefinitely, never
#      completing any of them. The bot got stuck at 110 supply with a
#      probe visibly "bouncing" at the pylon build location.
#
#      ROOT CAUSE: Same pattern as Dev Note #91 (PvP voidray main pylon
#      bounce). When the builder probe arrives at the pylon position and
#      build() is issued, the probe stands on/near the build site. On
#      the next frame, can_place() returns False because the probe is
#      blocking the position. The position gets blacklisted (via
#      _failed_build_positions), the task is cleared, and flower
#      production picks a NEW position. The probe walks to the new
#      position, same thing happens, and it bounces between two or more
#      positions forever.
#
#      The sticky pylon mechanism (Dev Note #108) partially mitigates
#      this by reusing the same position when a task is cleared. But
#      if the position is blacklisted by _failed_build_positions, the
#      sticky position is also cleared, and a new position is chosen.
#
#      FIX: Added _flower_pylon_ordered flag (same pattern as Dev Note
#      #91's _pvp_main_pylon_ordered). Once build() is successfully
#      issued for a pylon position, set this flag and skip can_place()
#      re-validation. Instead, check for:
#      (a) Pylon exists at the position (built or under construction) → done
#      (b) 5-second timeout → position truly blocked, re-search
#      (c) Builder probe died → clear and reassign
#      This prevents the probe-on-position → can_place fail → blacklist
#      → new position → bounce cycle.
# =========================================================================
#  124. PVT 86 SUPPLY BLOCK: GW_FIRST STATE 99 HANDOFF INFINITE LOOP:
#      The bot got stuck at 86 supply permanently. The builder probe was
#      assigned a pylon task but kept getting redirected away from the
#      pylon position.
#
#      ROOT CAUSE: When the gw_first state machine set state=99 (inactive)
#      and handed off the builder probe to the reaper response, the handoff
#      code in execute_pvt_gateway_first() ran EVERY FRAME because the
#      condition `opening_build_chosen != "gateway_first" and state >= 2`
#      was always True at state 99. This re-set _pvt_reaper_probe_tag
#      every frame, which the reaper state machine (state 10) then cleared
#      every frame, creating an infinite loop:
#        Frame A: gw_first re-sets _pvt_reaper_probe_tag → logs "gw_first state 99 → 99"
#        Frame B: reaper state 10 clears tag → logs "Releasing builder probe"
#        Frame C: gw_first re-sets tag again → ...
#      This kept builder_locked=True and prevented manage_flower_production
#      from ever building pylons.
#
#      FIX: Two changes:
#      (a) Added `state != 99` guard to the handoff condition so it only
#          runs once (when the handoff first happens).
#      (b) Added early return for state 99 so the function does nothing
#          after the recovery check, preventing any re-entry issues.
# =========================================================================
#  80. PVP VOIDRAY TARGETING PRIORITY: Voidrays were attacking zealots
#      instead of stalkers/archons/voidrays, causing them to die to
#      anti-air threats they could have killed first. In a PvP match,
#      the enemy attacked with 12 zealots + 2 stalkers + 1 immortal.
#      Our voidray targeted the closest enemy (zealots) while the 2
#      enemy stalkers shot it down. By t=360s, all our ground units
#      were dead and only 1 voidray remained.
#      ROOT CAUSE: Three issues in the voidray targeting code:
#      (a) The targeting priority was: voidrays (10 range) > stalkers
#          (10 range) > closest enemy. No archon priority existed at all.
#          The 10-range limit meant stalkers at 12+ range were invisible
#          to the priority system, so the voidray fell through to
#          "closest enemy" (usually a zealot) and attacked that instead.
#      (b) Archons are a critical threat to voidrays (bonus damage vs
#          biological, high HP, splash). They were never prioritized,
#          so a voidray would attack zealots while an archon walked up
#          and killed it.
#      (c) [RECURRING BUG] The zonal defense section (enemies within 25
#          of ourmain/ournat) bypassed the priority system entirely --
#          it just attacked closest_zonal enemy and continued, meaning
#          voidrays would hit zealots while stalkers shot them. The
#          priority code in the enemies_near_base section was never
#          reached because the zonal check came first and short-circuited
#          with `continue`.
#      FIX: In all three targeting paths (zonal defense, base defense,
#      and attack push), changed voidray targeting priority to:
#      voidrays > archons > stalkers > everything else. Removed the
#      10-range limit on all priority checks. Unified zonal and base
#      defense into a single vr_enemy_pool approach so the priority
#      system is ALWAYS applied -- zonal threats use zonal_enemies as
#      the pool, base threats use enemies_near_base as the pool, but
#      both go through the same priority: VRs > archons > stalkers > closest.
#      Affected code: _pvp_voidray_attack() defense and attack sections.
# =========================================================================
#  125. PVZ ROACH WARREN EARLY DEFENSE PROTOCOL:
#      When a roach warren is scouted early during the roach/bane build
#      (by ~3:13), the bot needs a specific defense protocol prioritizing
#      stalker mass and shield batteries over tech:
#
#      (a) STALKER-ONLY GATEWAY PRODUCTION: All gateways produce ONLY
#          stalkers. No zealots, no sentries, no adepts. Zealots are
#          melee and get kited by roaches. Sentries cost 100 gas and
#          delay stalker production. Stalkers are ranged and can kite.
#          The _pvz_early_rw flag forces pure stalker production from
#          ALL idle gateways every frame (not one-at-a-time).
#
#      (b) SHIELD BATTERY PRIORITY: Build shield batteries as the second
#          priority after stalker production. Batteries keep stalkers
#          alive during defense, allowing them to kite back and recharge
#          shields. At least 3 batteries should be built near the wall.
#
#      (c) BLOCK TWILIGHT AND WARPGATE UNTIL 10 STALKERS: The twilight
#          council costs 150/100 which delays stalker production. Warpgate
#          research costs 50/50 and gates behind cyber+twilight. Both are
#          delayed until we have at least 10 stalkers for defense. The
#          _pvz_early_rw_stalker_count tracks this. State 87 (twilight
#          build) and warpgate research both check this threshold.
#
#      (d) PROBE PRODUCTION GATED BY BATTERIES + GATEWAYS: Probes are
#          only produced when 3+ shield batteries are completed AND all
#          gateways are ready and not idle (actively producing stalkers).
#          Every probe costs 50 minerals which could be a stalker. The
#          _pvz_early_rw_probe_gate flag controls this in the worker
#          production code.
#
#      (e) CHRONO BOOST ONLY ON GATEWAYS: All chrono boost goes to
#          gateways producing stalkers. No chrono on nexus (probes),
#          twilight (charge), or robo (observer/immortal). Getting
#          stalkers out faster is the #1 priority. The chrono code in
#          manage_chrono step 3 checks _pvz_early_rw to redirect all
#          chrono to gateways.
#
#      (f) IDLE STALKERS DEFEND AT GW2: Idle stalkers (no enemies near)
#          should smart(right-click) the gateway unit closest to the gw2
#          position. This keeps stalkers clustered around gw2 instead of
#          spreading around the wall pylon. Smarting the gateway unit
#          (not the ground position) ensures stalkers path properly and
#          stay in a tight defensive formation. The army movement code
#          in the PvZ roach defense section uses this positioning.
# =========================================================================
#  126. PVZ FORGE KILL DEADLOCK ON ATTACK TIMING:
#      When opening forge-first (e.g. vs 12-pool), the wall has a forge+cannon
#      that must be destroyed to let the army move out. When attack timing
#      triggers (e.g. +1 weapons + charge done), the army should immediately
#      kill the forge+cannon to open the wall. Instead, TWO separate defense
#      mechanisms were pulling the army away to fight zerglings outside the wall:
#      (a) DEFENSE MODE (10+ enemies near base, army not committed): The army
#          would enter defense mode and attack zerglings through the wall gap.
#          Only ranged units could hit them, and the DPS was far too low to
#          clear them before reinforcements arrived. The army never reached
#          the forge destruction code.
#      (b) PVZ ZERGLING CHECK in the forge/cannon destruction code: Even if
#          the army somehow reached the forge destruction section, the code
#          explicitly refused to destroy the forge while zerglings were near
#          ournat, instead ordering the army to fight zerglings (through the
#          wall gap, again futilely) and returning early.
#      These two mechanisms created a circular deadlock: defense mode prevents
#      reaching forge destruction, and the zergling check prevents forge
#      destruction even when reached. Result: the forge wasn't destroyed until
#      t=590s (almost 4 minutes after attack timing at t=351s), while the army
#      helplessly shot at zerglings it couldn't kill through the wall.
#      FIX: Three changes:
#      (1) Set _wall_breakout_in_progress = True early in the attack timing
#          block, before the defense mode check, when a wall forge still exists.
#          This prevents defense mode from activating.
#      (2) Add _wall_breakout_in_progress check to the PvZ defense recall
#          execution, so the army isn't recalled while breaking out.
#      (3) Remove both zergling checks from the forge/cannon destruction code.
#          The priority is ALWAYS to open the wall — the army can't fight
#          effectively through the gap. Once the wall is open, the full army
#          can engage and the defense mechanisms will work normally.
#      Affected code: manage_army_movement() attack timing block.
# =========================================================================
#  127. SHIELD BATTERY PLACEMENT: EXCLUDE POSITIONS IN FRONT OF WALL
#      Shield batteries were being placed in front of the wall (closer to
#      the map center than the cyber core), making them useless for defense.
#      Batteries in front of the wall can't support stalkers retreating
#      from zerglings, and they get killed first by attacking forces.
#      FIX: Added a filter in both find_battery_near_wallpylon() and
#      find_all_battery_positions_near_wallpylon() that excludes any
#      candidate position whose distance to map center is less than the
#      cyber core's distance to map center. This ensures all batteries
#      are placed behind the wall (towards our base) where they can
#      actually support defensive operations.
#      Affected code: find_battery_near_wallpylon(), find_all_battery_positions_near_wallpylon()
# =========================================================================
#  128. PVT REAPER STATE 10 DEADLOCK WHEN FORGE IS SKIPPED:
#      When the forge position was invalid (state 8 logged a warning and
#      skipped it), state 10 would wait forever for a forge that doesn't
#      exist. The code was: `forges = self.structures(UnitTypeId.FORGE).ready;
#      if not forges: return`. This return prevents the charge research code
#      from ever executing. Without charge research, `_pvt_charge_started`
#      stays False, so `pvt_ready` is never True (it requires charge_done),
#      and the attack timing never triggers. Meanwhile, the bot floats 5000+
#      minerals with 5 idle warpgates because the entire game state is stuck.
#      FIX: In state 10, check if a forge exists or is pending. If neither,
#      set `_pvt_armor1_started = True` and `_pvt_forge_skipped = True` to
#      bypass the armor requirement, then proceed directly to charge research.
#      Also updated the `armor_gate` logic in `manage_army_movement()` to
#      check `_pvt_forge_skipped` — when the forge was skipped, the armor
#      gate is always True (no armor requirement), instead of waiting for
#      `armor1_done` which would never be True.
#      Affected code: execute_pvt_reaper_response() state 10, manage_army_movement() armor_gate
# =========================================================================
#  129. PVT SENTRY ANTI-CYCLONE MICRO: Sentries in the proxy hidden tech
#      path had no cyclone avoidance logic. A cyclone could freely lock
#      onto the sentry and kill it in 2-3 seconds. Sentries are slow
#      (3.5 speed vs stalker 4.13), cost 100 gas, and can't blink —
#      losing one to a lock-on is devastating on a 1-base economy.
#      FIX: Added anti-cyclone micro in the sentry leash section of
#      manage_pvt_stalker_defense. When a cyclone is within 15 of a
#      sentry and the sentry is within 10 of the cyclone, the sentry
#      moves 10 distance away from the cyclone. This keeps the sentry
#      outside lock-on range (~7). The check runs BEFORE the leash
#      check because avoiding lock-on is more important than staying
#      near the wall.
# =========================================================================
#  130. PVT STALKER ON-COOLDOWN STUTTER-STEP TOWARD CYCLONES/SIEGE TANKS:
#      During wall defense (pre-sweep, <5 stalkers), stalkers on weapon
#      cooldown just stood still while cyclones locked on from range and
#      siege tanks shelled them from 13 range. The defense Priority 1
#      and 2 sections only issued ATTACK orders with no on-cooldown
#      movement logic. Meanwhile, the sweep micro (Phase 1 and 2) had
#      proper on-cooldown handling (siege tank dive, cyclone approach).
#      FIX: Added on-cooldown stutter-step logic to wall defense
#      Priority 1 and 2. When a stalker is on weapon cooldown:
#      - Siege tanks: move TOWARD them (dive under minimum range where
#        they can't fire in siege mode)
#      - Cyclones: move TOWARD them (pressure and force flee; standing
#        still = free lock-on target)
#      - Other enemies within 7: kite back to 6 distance (max range)
#      - Enemies beyond 7: attack-move toward them
#      This ensures stalkers close the gap on dangerous ranged units
#      instead of being passive targets.
# =========================================================================
#  131. PVT BUILDER PROBE STUCK BUILDING SHIELD BATTERY AT BLOCKED POSITION:
#      The shield battery position (68, 146) was calculated from the wall
#      pylon, but wall structures (gw1, gw2, cybercore) physically blocked
#      the probe's pathing to within 1.5 of the target. The probe got
#      within 3-4 units (positions like 66,143 or 67,143) but couldn't
#      path any closer. The physical stuck detection replaced the probe
#      every 7 seconds, but each replacement also got stuck at the same
#      wall gap, creating an infinite replacement loop (11 replacements
#      observed in one game, never building the battery).
#      Additionally, once the 4-gate state machine transitioned from 5.6
#      to 10 (after issuing the build), it never checked if the battery
#      was actually built. If the build command was interrupted (by
#      combat, mining reassignment, or pathing failure), the battery
#      would never appear and the `_pvt_shield_battery_started` flag
#      prevented any retry.
#      FIX: Three changes:
#      (a) In manage_builder_probe_defense physical stuck detection: when
#          the builder is within 5 units of a battery target and stuck,
#          try issuing the build command from the current position. SC2
#          allows building from up to ~6-7 units away. If the build
#          fails 3+ times, clear the cached position for recalculation.
#      (b) In 4-gate state 5.6: increased build range from 1.5 to 5.0
#          for shield batteries. If the probe is within 5 units and
#          can_place succeeds, build immediately from the current
#          position instead of requiring the probe to walk closer.
#      (c) In 4-gate state 10: added check for missing shield battery.
#          If `_pvt_shield_battery_started` is True but no battery exists
#          and none is pending, the build failed — try to rebuild it
#          with position recalculation if the original position is now
#          blocked.
# =========================================================================
#  132. PVT FOUR DEFENSE BUGS (DEV NOTE #113):
#      (a) SENTRY ANTI-CYCLONE NOT RUNNING FOR NON-PROXY-HIDDEN-TECH:
#          The anti-cyclone micro from note #129 only ran inside the
