# =========================================================================
#  142. PVP NAT PYLON WRAP SCOUT: When the scout probe reaches the enemy
#      natural and spots an enemy pylon there, it must wrap around the pylon
#      at ~3 distance for up to 20 seconds to check for gateway or forge.
#      Without this wrap, the probe just continues on its queued path to
#      the enemy main and never sees what's next to the pylon. In the
#      reported game, the enemy had a gateway opener but it wasn't spotted
#      until 68s because the scout didn't investigate the pylon. The opener
#      detection then fell through to forge_first at 51s (no gateway/gas
#      visible), causing an incorrect emergency defense build.
#      BEHAVIOR:
#      (a) When scout reaches within 15 of enemynat and sees an enemy pylon
#          within 10 of enemynat, cancel queued orders and start orbiting
#          the pylon at radius 3 (8 orbit points).
#      (b) During orbit, detect_enemy_gateway() and detect_enemy_forge()
#          automatically flag structures as they enter vision.
#      (c) If gateway spotted → end wrap immediately (standard opener).
#      (d) If forge spotted → end wrap immediately (cannon rush defense).
#      (e) If 20 seconds pass with no gateway/forge → assume nexus first,
#          set opening to pvp_voidray, end wrap.
#      (f) After wrap ends, re-queue standard scouting path (enemymain
#          entry → circle → enemy3rd → circle) so the probe continues
#          its normal scouting duties.
#      (g) Prevents manage_scout_probe_timing() from interfering during
#          the wrap by checking _pvp_nat_pylon_wrap_active flag.
#      TIMING: The wrap starts when the pylon is first spotted (typically
#      ~34-35s) and lasts 20 seconds, ending around ~54-55s. The 51-second
#      decision logic in choose_opening_build() is NOT delayed to 71s.
#      Instead, if the wrap is still active at 51s, we simply skip the
#      51s check — the wrap will finish in a few seconds and handle the
#      decision. If no pylon was found by 51s (wrap never started), the
#      51s decision runs as the normal fallback.
# =========================================================================
#  143. PVP BATTERY POSITION MUST BE SNAPPED TO INTEGER GRID: Shield battery
#      is a 2x2 building, which means its position must be on integer
#      coordinates (e.g. (68, 140), not (68.04, 140.49)). The PvP battery
#      placement code (state 5 of _pvp_voidray_build) was calculating the
#      midpoint between gw1 and cyber, offsetting toward main, and storing
#      the raw float position without snapping. The game silently snaps it
#      when placing, but the code still references the unsnapped float,
#      causing a mismatch between the cached _pvp_battery_pos and the
#      actual battery position. This breaks distance checks used for
#      stalker micro (battery retreat, rally near battery, etc.).
#      FIX: Wrap all candidate positions with snap_to_integer_grid() in
#      the PvP battery placement code, same as PvT/PvZ battery placement.
# =========================================================================
#  144. PVP BATTERY POSITION: GRID-TILE ADJACENCY + BATTERY-CENTRIC LEASH
#      (a) Battery position was calculated using midpoint+offset (midpoint
#      between gw1 and cyber, offset toward main by 1.5, then snapped to
#      integer grid). This could be "off by 1 grid position" because
#      snap_to_integer_grid on a geometric midpoint doesn't account for how
#      2x2 building tiles actually sit on the grid relative to 3x3 buildings.
#      FIX: Replace midpoint+offset with grid-tile adjacency search (same
#      approach as find_temp_pylon_position and find_temp_cyber_position).
#      The new find_pvp_battery_position() method iterates integer-grid
#      candidates, checks that the 2x2 battery tiles are edge-adjacent to
#      both gw1 and cyber 3x3 tile sets, and is on the inside (main-facing)
#      side of the wall. This ensures the battery is snug against both
#      buildings at the actual grid level.
#      (b) Stalker positioning used NAT_LEASH=25 from natural as the leash
#      boundary, with idle stalkers rallying at 4-5 range TOWARD battery.
#      This meant stalkers would chase far from the battery and be outside
#      shielding range, and idle stalkers stood ON TOP of the battery
#      instead of between the battery and the enemy.
#      FIX: Changed to battery-centric leash: BATTERY_LEASH=7 (= shielding
#      distance). Stalkers beyond 7 from battery attack-move back. Idle
#      stalkers rally ~5 range toward map center from battery, putting them
#      between the enemy approach and the battery at the edge of shielding
#      range. When hurt, they still retreat to the battery for regeneration.
# =========================================================================
#  145. PVP SENTRY DOUBLE-TRAIN BUG: When the sentry training condition was
#      met (both stalkers done, sentry not yet trained, already_pending==0),
#      the for loop over idle gateways would train a sentry at the first idle
#      gateway, set _pvp_sentry_trained=True, but then continue to the next
#      idle gateway on the same frame. Since already_pending() reads from the
#      game state cache (not updated until next frame), the second gateway
#      also saw already_pending(SENTRY)==0 and trained a SECOND sentry. The
#      _pvp_sentry_trained flag only prevents the elif branch from running
#      again, but the "else" branch after it also has a sentry fallback that
#      checks sentries < 1 with the same stale already_pending count.
#      FIX: Break out of the gateway loop immediately after training a sentry.
#      This gives the game state one frame to update already_pending() before
#      any other gateway can attempt sentry production.
#      Also, _end_nat_pylon_wrap now parks the scout at
#      enemynat.towards(ournat, -10) instead of resuming the full enemymain
#      circle scouting path. This positions the probe ~10 units behind the
#      enemy mineral line on the side facing our base, giving vision of a
#      nexus being taken at the enemy natural (for nexus-first detection).
# =========================================================================
#  146. PVP SENTRY TIMING: CONDITIONAL ON ENEMY EXPANSION AND VOIDRAY
#      Old: Always train 2 stalkers then 1 sentry (3rd unit from gateways).
#      This was too rigid -- making a sentry before our first voidray when
#      the enemy hasn't expanded risks losing to pure stalkers (sentry costs
#      50 gas that could be a stalker, and sentry DPS is much lower).
#      New logic:
#      (a) Stalker 1 is always the first unit (non-negotiable defense).
#      (b) Stalker 2 slot: If enemy nexus is spotted at their natural →
#          train sentry instead of stalker 2 (enemy expanded = safe to get
#          sentry early for guardian shield). Otherwise → stalker 2.
#      (c) After initial 2 slots: sentry ONLY when we have a completed
#          voidray AND no sentry exists yet. No voidray = no sentry.
#      The enemy nat nexus check uses our parked scout (positioned at
#      enemynat.towards(ournat, -10)) to check enemy_structures(NEXUS)
#      within 15 of enemynat.
# =========================================================================
#  148. PVT TWILIGHT COUNCIL PROBE BOUNCE: The probe assigned to build the
#      twilight council was bouncing between mining and the build target.
#      ROOT CAUSE: manage_production_buildings() used `if not self.builder_locked:
#      self.builder_probe_tag = worker.tag` when assigning the twilight builder.
#      When builder_locked was True (state machine holding a different probe),
#      the new worker got builder_current_task="twilight" but NOT builder_probe_tag.
#      manage_complex_mining() excludes workers via builder_probe_tag in away_tags.
#      Without it, the twilight builder was NOT excluded → got HARVEST_GATHER
#      → is_builder_busy() saw mining → cleared the twilight task → worker went
#      back to mining → manage_production_buildings() reassigned → bounce loop.
#      FIX (3 parts):
#      (a) ALWAYS set builder_probe_tag when assigning twilight builder,
#          regardless of builder_locked. State machines use their own tags
#          (_pvt_reaper_probe_tag, etc.) and don't depend on builder_probe_tag.
#      (b) Added `not (self.builder_current_task == "twilight")` guard in
#          manage_production_buildings() to prevent reassigning every frame
#          when already_pending() returns 0 (stale within same frame, same
#          bug as sentry double-train #145).
#      (c) When is_builder_busy() clears the twilight task (hijacked, idle,
#          stuck, retries exhausted), also clear builder_probe_tag so mining
#          can reclaim the worker immediately (prevents ghost lock).
#      Also expanded the PvP guard in manage_production_buildings() from
#      `opening_build_chosen == "pvp_voidray"` to all PvP openings
#      (`self.vs_protoss`), since PvP never builds twilight (voidray path).
# =========================================================================
